+# Send pickup reminders by SMS
+
+For whoever is next asked to switch pickup reminders to email to save the SMS cost. Reminders stay on SMS because
+email reminders raised missed pickups, and every missed pickup is a packed bag whose fresh food goes in the bin by
+closing time.
+
+## Context
+
+SMS reminders cost larder about 40 euros a month. Pickup reminders went out by email instead from April to June
+2025 to save that cost. The email provider's own report found 34% of the reminder emails were never opened, and
+missed pickups rose from 6% of pantry orders to 13% over that period. Many members share one phone in the family and
+rarely check email, but they do see an SMS. Larder switched back to SMS on July 1, 2025, and missed pickups returned
+to 6% by August.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Email**: free; tried April-June 2025 and lost, missed pickups roughly doubled because over a third of reminders
+  went unopened.
+- **SMS**: costs about 40 euros a month; chosen, because members who share a phone in the family still see a text.
+
+## Decision
+
+Pickup reminders stay on SMS. The 40 euros a month is cheaper than the fresh food lost to missed pickups.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Switching reminders to email repeats the April-June 2025 regression: expect missed pickups to climb back from 6%
+toward 13% as emails go unopened. Any future proposal to drop SMS needs a way to reach members who share a phone and
+do not check email, not just a cheaper channel.
